format session-log-v1
stamp 20260821.185411
editor Claude Code
model claude-opus-5
voice Kyri
host Vultr SEA NixOS VPS ai-jail pier (Daylight DC-1 hand) -- Eastern (EDT)
title The spelling decision applied at last, and the record kept coherent
prompt do the colour normalization -- didnt a while ago we seat color over colour molt breach?
think Went looking for the seat before touching anything, because whether a decision already exists changes what I am allowed to do rather than merely how I describe it.
think Found it: external-research/20260816-221015_standfast-correction-and-the-decision-wave.md names "the color decision" -- American spelling, normalize on touch, seated 20260816.221015. Keaton remembered right. It was a RATCHET rather than a molt or a breach, which is the precise answer to the question asked.
think Found the precedent for dated artifacts too. Commit 291c7fa6da normalized a session log IN PLACE under this same directive, and scoped itself to only the files that round touched. So body normalization of dated testimony is established practice here, and the tree-wide application was simply never done.
think Refused to treat this as a spelling sweep after one grep. 680 occurrences across 125 files is not prose alone, and the first look found craft_colour as a function name and colour:red inside a query string. Identifiers and data are not comments.
think Read craft_colour rather than renaming it on sight. It is a file-local helper repeated in six modules, each defining and calling its own copy, so every rename is self-contained and no cross-module surface moves.
think Read colour:red rather than assuming it was a label. It is a NEGATIVE test in sixteen files: the parser knows material: and max:, so colour is bad because the KEY is unknown. Which means color is equally unknown and the behavior is identical -- but if color had happened to be a known key, the rename would have silently turned a refusal into an acceptance and the test would still have passed. That is the whole argument for looking.
think Caught the trap that would have made this round eat itself. Four surfaces state or record the rule -- REMEMBER carries the directive in the exact words "Use color, not colour", REDS carries %114's description of the drift, the external-research note IS the seat, and the index plus three of this arc's own logs describe it. A normalization that cannot describe what it normalized has erased its own reason.
think Held those out by name rather than by a clever pattern. A regex that tries to tell "a rule about spelling" from "a use of the spelling" is a regex that will be wrong on the file that matters most.
think Renamed the three dated filenames only after measuring who cites them, and each was cited by exactly one file: the index I control. So the rename is total rather than partial, and no reference is left unrecoverable -- which mattered here, since a rename changes the basename and the basename is the resolver's key.
obs Ran all 57 witnesses over the touched modules rather than a sample, because the sweep edited 74 code files and a spelling change in a print string and a spelling change in an identifier look identical in a diff. PASS 57, FAIL 0.
obs The census held at lost=193 through three basename renames, which is the resolver's own claim tested rather than assumed.
obs REDS crossed its byte bound again and rows 113-114 folded to archive. Third fold of that ledger today; the pin is small and the record is whole.
loom sweep=colour_to_color files=123 occurrences=799 held_out=4_rule_surfaces third_party_excluded=gratitude+vendor+seed
loom renames=dated_logs count=3 cited_by=index_only census_lost_before=193 after=193
loom witnesses=touched_modules run=57 pass=57 fail=0
file crux/REDS.md %114 CLOSED -- what had to be read, and the four surfaces held out
file session-logs/date/20260817/20260817-093701_text-grid-per-cell-color.kyri renamed with git mv, history whole
file crux/archive/REDS-fold-round-rows-113-114.md rows folded to keep the pin under bound
recommend check-in (checkpoint) the spelling decision seated 20260816.221015 is now applied across the tree, and no known red stands on this pier. The four held-out surfaces keep the word deliberately, since each of them is the record of why the sweep happened. Remaining colour in gratitude/ and vendor/ is third-party text and stays exactly as its authors wrote it.
